.chapter 6
.h1 Testing [testing]
The project has five kinds of test and one rule for every check: it must fail
when its feature is off.  One script runs the tests, with a part for each kind.
.h2 Kinds of test [testing-kinds]
{Tab:kinds} lists the kinds.  The first tests used the 24 sample documents of
the old program.  On 17 November documents made for the project replaced them:
a community orchard and its plan, brief and yearly report, a utility that
prints tide tables, and letters and memos of a club.
.tabcap kinds Kinds of test
.table 14 50
Kind | Method
Unit | A program links the code and calls it with exact expected values.  The
 | loader test loads each damaged document in a child process, so a crash or a
 | hang fails one check and not the run.
Terminal | The Editor runs in a pseudo-terminal.  The test sends keys, waits
 | until a text is on the screen and reads the screen as text.
X11 and pixel | The X11 Editor runs on a virtual display; {c:xdotool} sends
 | keys and {c:xwd} captures the window.  Checks cover borders and colours.
PDF | {c:pdftotext} reads the PDF of a print.  A page converted at 300 or 400
 | dpi checks unbroken lines, colours and the position of a word in points.
Manual | The build checks keycaps, the border rule of tables and the three pages
 | of the leaflet of keys.  Pictures are retaken and compared.
.end
.h2 Mutation rule [testing-mutation]
A check that passes while its feature does nothing is vacuous.  It is worse
than no check, because it reports the feature as tested.  Mutation testing
finds it: one line of the program is changed so that the feature is lost, the
program must still build with all warnings as errors, the test runs, and the
line is put back.  A check that does not fail is rewritten.  {Tab:mutations}
lists results of the first audit.
.tabcap mutations Examples of mutations and their effect on the tests
.table 16 26 20
Test of | Mutation | Result
Header and footer | header and footer not rendered | 12 of 19 fail: caught
Index and lists | index never made | 7 of 18 fail: caught
Index and lists | lists hold figures and tables | 0 fail: vacuous, fixed
Calculator | rounding at the 101st digit half down | 0 fail: vacuous, fixed
.end
The check of the list of figures looked only for figure names, so a list of
every kind passed; one check per list now requires that the other kind is
absent.  No calculator expression had a cut-off digit of exactly 5, so
rounding down at the half passed; two expressions were added.  Commit messages
give the mutation and the number of failed checks under Testing.
.h2 Counts [testing-counts]
The verification before a merge builds, runs the unit tests, the smoke and the
X11 tests, and checks that they left the source tree unchanged.  {Tab:counts}
gives the result of 19 November.  The complete smoke printed no line that
begins with "FAIL", in @smoke_seconds@ s.
.tabcap counts Checks of the verification of 19 November
.table 51 12
Part | Checks
The unit tests (sixteen programs that print a number) | @unit_checks@
The complete smoke: terminal scripts, the Printer, the Viewer | @smoke_ok@
The X11 smoke | @xsmoke_ok@
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h2 Faulty tests [testing-flakes]
A test that fails without a fault in the program is flaky and teaches people to
ignore failures.  Two kinds were found.
Fixed waits: the parallel smoke on a machine with eight busy loops failed five
checks of the Viewer and eight of the Print panel.  The programs were right and
the tests too quick.  A step that waits for a text on the screen, for up to 20
s, replaced the fixed waits.
Temporary files: programs ignored {c:TMPDIR}, and a test with a long path
failed in several places, among them a socket path above the 107 characters
that the system allows.  Programs now use the folder that {c:TMPDIR} names, and
a quick test runs with a private empty folder and fails when a program leaves
anything in it.
.endif
